# audiohub-graph-v2.1 — always-on multi-output audio hub, Raspberry Pi (KMS)
#
# ⚠ UNTESTED — THIS GRAPH HAS NEVER BEEN PLAYED. Written at the desk on
# 2026-09-12 (pi-tools#t-009) because no KMS Pi exists on the bench to hear it
# on, and shipped anyway on an explicit call: a missing file costs more than an
# unverified one, since without it a KMS Pi gets asound.conf-pi3 and a
# DANGLING hdmiout. Treat every line below as a PROPOSAL derived from
# asound.conf-pi3, not as a verified config. The first person to reach a KMS Pi
# should read "What to check first" at the bottom before debugging anything.
#
# WHEN THIS FILE IS THE RIGHT ONE: a Pi booted with `dtoverlay=vc4-kms-v3d`
# (what setup/bootstrap.py's own config.txt writes) — `/proc/asound/cards`
# shows a `vc4hdmi*` card. The RastaOS 7.x golden image is NOT this: it stays
# on the legacy dispmanx/mmal stack (`arm_64bit=0`, no vc4-kms — KMS performs
# badly there) and uses asound.conf-pi3. This graph serves hand-installed KMS
# Pis only; install.sh picks between the two from the cards it finds.
#
# Same design as asound.conf-pi3 (see that header for the full rationale):
# the player application plays pcm.hplayer (plug pinned to snd-aloop at
# 8ch/48k), every physical output is an alsaloop forwarder reading the shared
# dsnoop capture:
#
#   app --> plug --> hw:Loopback,0,0             (fixed 8ch / 48k / S16)
#                        |
#                 hw:Loopback,1,0 --> dsnoop "aloopcap"
#                        |-- alsaloop --> jackout   (Headphones, DOWNMIX, always)
#                        |-- alsaloop --> hdmiout   (vc4hdmi0, ch 0/1, always,
#                        |                           softvol mute)
#                        `-- alsaloop --> usbout<N>:CARD=<id>  (hotplug, STRAIGHT)
#
# WHAT CHANGES vs asound.conf-pi3 — the KMS split, and nothing else:
#
#   * HDMI is no longer bcm2835. The legacy firmware stack registered one card
#     per HDMI port (`b1` = HDMI0, `b2` = HDMI1); vc4-kms-v3d registers the
#     vc4-hdmi driver instead, as `vc4hdmi0`/`vc4hdmi1` on a two-port Pi 4/5
#     and as plain `vc4hdmi` on a single-port Pi 3. hdmiout targets
#     **vc4hdmi0** — the first physical port, which is also the one
#     setup/bootstrap.py's config.txt drives (`video=HDMI-A-1`). House
#     convention, same as asound.conf-x86's device-3 pick: the file hardcodes
#     the first port, and you EDIT IT for another one. install.sh says so out
#     loud when the card it found is not the card named here (the Pi 3 case).
#   * The analog jack does not change. `dtparam=audio=on` keeps the analog
#     half of bcm2835 under KMS, still card `Headphones` — so jackout, its
#     fold-down ttable and ctl.!default are byte-identical to the pi3 graph.
#     ASSUMED, not measured: if `/proc/asound/cards` names it anything else on
#     a real box, jackout is a one-line fix here.
#   * usbout2/4/6/8 are byte-identical. USB audio has nothing to do with the
#     display stack.
#
# WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE, and why the hub shape is kept as-is: the dmix/multi
# breakage that motivated the loopback hub was measured on the bcm2835
# firmware devices (bench 2026-07-21, player-000), so it is not itself a
# reason to keep the hub on a vc4 HDMI path — but failure isolation, the one
# deterministic latency target, and one app-facing PCM across every fleet are,
# exactly as on x86. Nothing here is ever rewritten at runtime; the
# "audiohub-graph-v2.1" marker on line 1 is the deploy check.

# resampler for plug conversions (USB cards stuck at 44.1k, e.g. ESI
# GIGAPort HD+ whose only altset is 8ch/44100): alsa-lib's default linear
# converter audibly aliases — speexrate is clean and ARM-friendly. Needs
# libasound2-plugins (a module dep). 48k-capable cards convert nothing.
defaults.pcm.rate_converter "speexrate_medium"

# ---- app-facing playback PCM ---------------------------------------------
# Unchanged from asound.conf-pi3: the slave is pinned so the loopback cable
# always carries 8ch/48k/S16 whatever the media layout, which means the
# dsnoop side below can never hit a parameter conflict.

pcm.hplayer {
    type plug
    slave {
        pcm "hw:Loopback,0,0"
        channels 8
        rate 48000
        format S16_LE
    }
}

pcm.!default hplayer
ctl.!default {
    type hw
    card Headphones
}

# ---- shared capture side for the forwarders ------------------------------
# Small periods (delivery granularity), DEEP buffer (stall absorption) —
# see asound.conf-pi3 for the measured rationale.

# NOTE: dsnoop's shared-memory instance outlives its readers — geometry
# edits below are IGNORED by a live system until the ipc_key changes.
# Bump the key together with any period/buffer change.
#
# The key is deliberately the SAME 6202 as the pi3 and x86 graphs: the
# geometry is identical, and a box only ever has one of the three installed.
pcm.aloopcap {
    type dsnoop
    ipc_key 6202
    slave {
        pcm "hw:Loopback,1,0"
        channels 8
        rate 48000
        format S16_LE
        period_size 256
        buffer_size 16384
    }
}

# ---- per-output egress PCMs ----------------------------------------------

# jack: ch0/1 unity + all other channels folded at 0.5 — the only output that
# COLLAPSES, so a multichannel piece is audible on a bench headphone. Sums
# saturate (clip) rather than wrap: monitoring output, not a mix bus.
# Byte-identical to asound.conf-pi3 (same bcm2835 analog card under KMS).
pcm.jackout {
    type route
    slave.pcm {
        type plug
        slave.pcm "hw:Headphones"
    }
    slave.channels 2
    ttable.0.0 1
    ttable.1.1 1
    ttable.2.0 0.5
    ttable.3.1 0.5
    ttable.4.0 0.5
    ttable.5.1 0.5
    ttable.6.0 0.5
    ttable.7.1 0.5
}

# hdmi: ch0/1 straight, through a softvol mute stage — same design as
# asound.conf-pi3, only the card changed (b1 -> vc4hdmi0). The "Audiohub HDMI"
# control is anchored on the always-present Loopback card and only materializes
# at the first hdmiout open: audiohub-fwd opens the PCM at unit start and
# applies the persisted state (mute=hdmi in /data/audiohub.conf, toggled with
# `audiohub mute|unmute hdmi`) BEFORE any audio can flow. resolution 2 makes it
# a SWITCH (on = untouched passthrough, off = hard-zeroed samples — drive it
# with on/off, never percentages); muted, alsaloop keeps streaming zeros, so
# the display keeps its audio stream and hw_ptr flow-watching stays valid.
#
# UNVERIFIED, and the likeliest thing to bite: vc4-hdmi carries its own
# constraints that the bcm2835 firmware device did not. Expect an open to need
# a CONNECTED display (an unplugged or ELD-less port is the x86 HDA situation,
# documented in asound.conf-x86 — there a port without an ELD refuses opens),
# and expect the accepted rate/channel set to follow the sink's ELD rather
# than being fixed. The plug + route pair below is what absorbs that: the
# forwarder always writes 8ch/48k and the route folds to 2ch, so a sink that
# wants stereo 48k gets exactly that. If HDMI is silent on a real KMS Pi, the
# first question is whether `hw:vc4hdmi0` opens AT ALL (see the bottom).
pcm.hdmiout {
    type route
    slave.pcm {
        type softvol
        slave.pcm {
            type plug
            slave.pcm "hw:vc4hdmi0"
        }
        control {
            name "Audiohub HDMI"
            card Loopback
        }
        resolution 2
    }
    slave.channels 2
    ttable.0.0 1
    ttable.1.1 1
}

# usb: STRAIGHT identity routes, one per card width — the wrapper picks.
# (plug's automatic 8->2 conversion would DOWNMIX, folding zones into the
# stereo pair: exactly what these explicit identity tables prevent.)
# Byte-identical to asound.conf-pi3.

pcm.usbout2 {
    @args [ CARD ]
    @args.CARD { type string }
    type route
    slave.pcm {
        type plug
        slave.pcm { type hw; card $CARD }
    }
    slave.channels 2
    ttable.0.0 1
    ttable.1.1 1
}

pcm.usbout4 {
    @args [ CARD ]
    @args.CARD { type string }
    type route
    slave.pcm {
        type plug
        slave.pcm { type hw; card $CARD }
    }
    slave.channels 4
    ttable.0.0 1
    ttable.1.1 1
    ttable.2.2 1
    ttable.3.3 1
}

pcm.usbout6 {
    @args [ CARD ]
    @args.CARD { type string }
    type route
    slave.pcm {
        type plug
        slave.pcm { type hw; card $CARD }
    }
    slave.channels 6
    ttable.0.0 1
    ttable.1.1 1
    ttable.2.2 1
    ttable.3.3 1
    ttable.4.4 1
    ttable.5.5 1
}

pcm.usbout8 {
    @args [ CARD ]
    @args.CARD { type string }
    type route
    slave.pcm {
        type plug
        slave.pcm { type hw; card $CARD }
    }
    slave.channels 8
    ttable.0.0 1
    ttable.1.1 1
    ttable.2.2 1
    ttable.3.3 1
    ttable.4.4 1
    ttable.5.5 1
    ttable.6.6 1
    ttable.7.7 1
}

# ---- legacy names kept for hand tools (aplay -D jack ...) ----------------

pcm.jack {
    type hw
    card Headphones
}

ctl.jack {
    type hw
    card Headphones
}

pcm.hdmi0 {
    type hw
    card vc4hdmi0
}

ctl.hdmi0 {
    type hw
    card vc4hdmi0
}

# ---- What to check first, on the first real KMS Pi -----------------------
#
# In this order — each step is a fact this file had to guess:
#
#   1. `cat /proc/asound/cards`. Confirm the analog card really is
#      `Headphones` and read the HDMI card's exact id. If it is `vc4hdmi`
#      (single-port Pi 3) or `vc4hdmi1` (second port), edit the three
#      `vc4hdmi0` occurrences above. audiohub-fwd resolves this one at
#      runtime and does NOT need editing.
#   2. `aplay -D hdmi0 -f S16_LE -r 48000 -c 2 /dev/zero` with a display
#      CONNECTED. This bypasses the hub entirely: if the raw card will not
#      open, nothing in this graph can help, and the answer is in
#      `dmesg | grep vc4`.
#   3. `audiohub test hdmi`, then `audiohub test jack`. That is the graph.
#   4. `audiohub mute hdmi` / `unmute hdmi` — the softvol control is anchored
#      on Loopback, so it should behave exactly as on the pi3 and x86 graphs.
#   5. Then, and only then, delete the ⚠ block at the top of this file and
#      record what you measured — the way asound.conf-pi3's header does.
#
# Coordinates for the record: pi-tools#t-003 is the bench task that owns this
# verification on aarch64 hardware.
